SAT_CHUNK("rw/central-ideas/2/1",[
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5f968ba99d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-0","passage":"<p>For decades, farmers in one river valley burned the stubble left in their rice fields after each harvest. Burning was fast and cheap, but it filled the air with smoke for weeks. When a local cooperative offered to buy the stubble instead, many farmers were skeptical that anyone would want it. Within three years, however, the cooperative was turning the straw into packaging material and selling it to companies across the region, and the autumn smoke had largely disappeared.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Farmers in the valley have stopped growing rice because burning stubble became too expensive.","Most farmers in the valley still prefer to burn their rice stubble after each harvest.","A cooperative's decision to buy rice stubble gave farmers an alternative to burning it and reduced smoke in the valley.","Packaging made from straw is stronger than packaging made from other materials."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text moves from the old practice (burning) to the cooperative's offer and its results: straw became packaging and the smoke largely disappeared. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ff46e57f22","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-3","passage":"<p>When the city opened its first public library branch in a converted bus station, officials expected it to be used mostly for borrowing books. Instead, the busiest part of the building turned out to be a row of tables with electrical outlets. Students came to charge laptops and do homework, job seekers used the free internet to fill out applications, and retirees gathered to read newspapers. Within a year, the library had added twice as many tables and cut back its shelf space.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Students prefer libraries that are located in former bus stations.","A new library branch became valued more as a place to work and gather than as a place to borrow books.","The city's first library branch failed to attract many visitors.","Borrowing books has become more popular than using the internet at libraries."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The unexpected popularity of tables and outlets led the library to add tables and cut shelf space. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5d06e7217e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-5","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The storm had taken the power at dusk, and by nine the house was silent except for the wind. Jalen's little sister had stopped crying an hour ago, mostly because he had turned the flashlight into a stage light and was performing every animal voice he knew. He ran out of animals around eleven. By then she was asleep, and he sat in the dark holding the flashlight, listening to the windows rattle and wishing someone would do the same for him.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Jalen comforts his sister during a frightening storm, even though he is also frightened.","Jalen enjoys storms because they give him a chance to perform.","Jalen's sister entertains him with animal voices until he falls asleep.","Jalen is annoyed that his sister will not stop crying during the storm."],"answer":"A","explanation":"He entertains her until she sleeps, then sits \"wishing someone would do the same for him.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f7aaf57596","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-6","passage":"<p>For most of the twentieth century, many linguists regarded the \"uptalk\" pattern, in which statements end with a rising pitch, as a sign of uncertainty. More recent studies complicate this view. In recorded conversations, speakers who used rising pitch often did so while holding the floor, signaling that they had more to say, or while checking that listeners were following along. Rather than expressing doubt, the rise frequently seemed to invite engagement.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rising pitch is used more often in writing than in speech.","Speakers who use rising pitch are usually unsure of what they are saying.","Linguists have stopped studying the way people speak in conversations.","Recent research suggests that rising pitch at the end of statements often serves purposes other than expressing uncertainty."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text contrasts the older view (uncertainty) with newer findings (holding the floor, checking understanding). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.67fc746f54","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-7","passage":"<p>In 1928, the town of Eastbridge replaced its wooden footbridge with a concrete one. The new bridge was stronger, but residents soon noticed that the swallows that had nested beneath the old bridge for generations were gone. The birds' mud nests could not cling to the smooth concrete. In 2019, volunteers attached rough wooden panels to the underside of the bridge. The following spring, the swallows returned.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Wooden bridges are generally stronger than concrete bridges.","The town of Eastbridge rebuilt its bridge in 2019 using wood.","Swallows left a bridge when its surface became too smooth for their nests and returned after rough panels were added.","Swallows prefer to nest near rivers rather than near towns."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text links the smooth concrete to the swallows' departure and the rough panels to their return. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d79769911b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-8","passage":"<p>Economists sometimes describe a \"cobra effect,\" in which a policy produces the opposite of its intended result. The term comes from a story, possibly exaggerated, about a reward once offered for dead cobras to reduce their numbers. Some residents reportedly began breeding cobras to collect the reward. When officials canceled the program, the breeders released their now-worthless snakes, leaving more cobras than before. Whether or not every detail is accurate, economists use the story to show how incentives can backfire.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The \"cobra effect\" illustrates how a policy's incentives can lead to results opposite to those intended.","Governments should never offer rewards to reduce animal populations.","Breeding snakes is a common way to earn money in many countries.","Economists have proven that the cobra story is entirely true."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The story shows a reward meant to reduce cobras ending up increasing them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5ba4745786","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-10","passage":"<p>Octopuses have a remarkable ability to taste with their arms. Each arm is lined with suckers containing thousands of chemical receptors, allowing an octopus to detect prey hidden in crevices simply by touching the surrounding rock. Researchers have also found that an arm can begin reacting to a promising scent before the octopus's central brain appears to direct it, suggesting that each arm processes some information on its own.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Octopuses rely mainly on eyesight to locate prey.","An octopus's central brain controls every movement of its arms.","Octopuses taste food only after bringing it to their mouths.","Octopus arms contain chemical receptors that let them sense prey and may process some information independently."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text describes receptors in the suckers and arms that react before the central brain seems to direct them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9b682badc1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-12","passage":"<p>Glass frogs, found in Central and South American rain forests, have translucent skin on their undersides. For years, scientists assumed this transparency simply helped the frogs blend in with the leaves they rest on. A recent study found something more surprising: while sleeping, a glass frog moves most of its red blood cells into its liver, making its body far more transparent. When the frog wakes and becomes active, the blood cells return to circulation.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Glass frogs live mostly on the forest floor rather than on leaves.","Glass frogs are completely invisible during the day.","Glass frogs lose their red blood cells permanently as they age.","Glass frogs become more transparent while sleeping by storing most of their red blood cells in their liver."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The study found that the frogs hide blood cells in the liver while sleeping, increasing transparency. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.53d03e5b45","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-15","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather's garden was a map of his life. The fig tree by the gate came from a cutting he had carried across an ocean wrapped in a damp shirt. The roses along the fence had been a gift on the day my mother was born. And the stubborn patch of mint, which he pulled up every spring and which grew back every summer, he said reminded him of himself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator's grandfather dislikes the mint that grows in his garden.","The garden contains only plants brought from another country.","The narrator's mother planted most of the garden.","The narrator's grandfather sees the plants in his garden as connected to important parts of his life."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Each plant is linked to a memory or trait, making the garden \"a map of his life.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f0bdb09390","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-17","passage":"<p>Early mapmakers often filled the unexplored edges of their maps with drawings of sea monsters. Historians once treated these images as simple decoration or evidence of superstition. One historian has proposed a different reading: many of the creatures closely resemble descriptions from sailors' reports of whales, walruses, and giant squid. On this view, the drawings were mapmakers' attempts, however imperfect, to record real animals that travelers had described.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Historians agree that sea monsters on maps were purely decorative.","Early mapmakers invented sea monsters to frighten sailors away from certain waters.","Early sailors never encountered whales or giant squid.","One historian argues that sea monsters on early maps may represent attempts to depict real animals described by sailors."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text presents the historian's reading that the drawings recorded real animals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.888f663df9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-19","passage":"<p>Scientists studying tree rings from a forest in a dry region found that rings formed during years of heavy rainfall were noticeably wider than rings from drought years. By measuring rings in trees that were hundreds of years old, the team reconstructed a record of rainfall stretching back far earlier than any written weather records from the area. The reconstruction revealed several long droughts that had not previously been documented.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Written weather records from the area go back hundreds of years.","Tree rings allowed scientists to reconstruct a long rainfall record and identify previously unknown droughts.","Trees in dry regions grow faster during droughts than during wet years.","Scientists planted new trees to measure future rainfall."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Ring widths reflected rainfall, allowing a reconstruction that revealed undocumented droughts. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3509ff6968","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data1-20","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father never told me he was proud of me. He was not a man who said such things. But every newspaper clipping that mentioned my name, even a single line about a spelling bee, ended up in a folder in his desk drawer. I found the folder after he died. It was two inches thick.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator won many spelling bees as a child.","The narrator's father disapproved of her achievements.","The narrator discovers after her father's death evidence of the pride he never expressed aloud.","The narrator kept newspaper clippings about her father."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The folder of clippings shows the pride her father \"never told\" her about. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f13ab7992c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-29","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, the carnival arrived in town on the same Tuesday and left on the same Sunday. Nadia had watched it come and go for eleven years without ever riding the Ferris wheel. This year, standing in line with a ticket that had cost her two weeks of saved allowance, she felt her heart pounding. When the attendant waved her forward, she stepped into the swaying seat before she could change her mind.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Nadia decides not to ride the Ferris wheel after all.","Nadia works at the carnival every summer.","Nadia overcomes her hesitation and rides the Ferris wheel for the first time.","Nadia is disappointed that the carnival arrives late this year."],"answer":"C","explanation":"After eleven years of not riding, she steps into the seat \"before she could change her mind.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.95da1b0fa2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-30","passage":"<p>For centuries, people in some mountain villages have built terraces, flat steps carved into steep hillsides, to grow crops. Terraces slow the flow of rainwater so that it soaks into the soil instead of rushing downhill and carrying the soil away. In several regions where terraces were abandoned as families moved to cities, landslides and erosion increased within a few decades.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Terraces protect hillside soil by slowing rainwater, and abandoning them can lead to erosion and landslides.","Mountain villages have stopped growing crops entirely.","Terraces are a recent invention used mainly in cities.","Rainwater erodes flat land more quickly than steep land."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The text explains how terraces slow water and what happened when they were abandoned. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1833af63da","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-32","passage":"<p>Scientists once assumed that honeybees navigate mainly by remembering landmarks. Experiments have shown that bees also rely on the position of the sun, and they can compensate for the sun's movement across the sky over the course of a day. Even when clouds hide the sun, bees can detect patterns of polarized light in patches of blue sky, allowing them to keep their bearings.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Honeybees cannot find their way when the sky is cloudy.","Honeybees navigate using the sun's position and polarized light, not only landmarks.","Honeybees fly only during the middle of the day.","Honeybees navigate only by remembering landmarks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text adds the sun and polarized light to the earlier assumption about landmarks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3305e30f2e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-34","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century letter.</p><p>My dear sister, the voyage has been long, and the sea has not been kind. For nine days the waves rose higher than the mast, and I confess I did not expect to write to you again. Yet this morning the sky cleared, a flock of gulls circled the ship, and the captain says we shall see land within two days. I have never been so glad to hear the cry of a bird.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The writer describes a peaceful and uneventful trip.","The writer regrets leaving home and plans to return immediately.","After a frightening voyage, the writer feels relief as signs indicate that land is near.","The writer is annoyed by the noise of the gulls."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The storm made the writer fear the worst; the clear sky, gulls, and news of land bring relief. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5f5b3ffdea","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-37","passage":"<p>A marine biologist studying cleaner wrasses, small fish that eat parasites off larger fish, noticed that the wrasses behaved differently when other fish were watching. When observed by potential clients, the wrasses were more careful to eat only parasites rather than nipping at the healthy mucus on their clients' skin, which they prefer. The biologist suggests that the wrasses may be managing their reputations.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Cleaner wrasses eat only mucus and never parasites.","Cleaner wrasses appear to behave more cooperatively when other fish can see them, suggesting a concern with reputation.","Cleaner wrasses live alone and rarely interact with other fish.","Larger fish avoid cleaner wrasses whenever possible."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Their behavior changes when observed, which the biologist links to reputation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.80c391b5fe","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data2-38","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>For three generations the Okonkwo family had run the hardware store on Main Street, and for three generations the bell above its door had rung the same flat note. When Adaeze took over the shop, she replaced the dusty shelves, repainted the walls a pale green, and installed bright new lights. But when a customer suggested she replace the bell, she shook her head. Some things, she said, should sound exactly the way they always have.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["While modernizing the family store, Adaeze chooses to preserve the old bell because of its connection to the past.","Customers dislike the changes Adaeze has made.","Adaeze plans to sell the hardware store.","Adaeze refuses to make any changes to the store."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She renovates the store but keeps the bell, which has rung the same note for three generations. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3bb636bdf1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-39","passage":"<p>When the town of Millbrook lost its only grocery store, residents who did not own cars faced a forty-minute bus ride to buy fresh food. A group of retirees responded by organizing a weekly market in the church parking lot, inviting farmers from nearby counties to sell produce directly. The market began with four vendors. Five years later it hosts thirty, and the town council has voted to make the parking lot a permanent public market space.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The town council built a new grocery store to replace the one that closed.","Millbrook's residents prefer shopping at farmers' markets to shopping at grocery stores.","After losing its grocery store, a town gained access to fresh food through a market that residents organized and that has since grown.","Farmers from nearby counties opened a grocery store in Millbrook."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text moves from the loss of the store to the retirees' market and its growth and official recognition. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.464e6791be","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-40","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every evening at six, Mr. Hadley walked the length of Harbor Road with his hands clasped behind his back, nodding to each neighbor by name. When he fell ill one winter and the walks stopped, no one mentioned it at first. But by the third evening, doors along Harbor Road began to open, and one neighbor after another found a reason to carry soup, or a newspaper, or simply a question, to the small blue house at the end of the street.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The neighbors' response to Mr. Hadley's absence shows how much his daily presence meant to them.","The neighbors on Harbor Road rarely spoke to one another.","Mr. Hadley moved away from Harbor Road during the winter.","Mr. Hadley's neighbors did not notice when he stopped taking his evening walks."],"answer":"A","explanation":"When his walks stop, the neighbors come to his house with soup and newspapers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6dd27d2da8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-41","passage":"<p>Many people picture icebergs as white, but some are strikingly blue. The color comes from ice that formed under great pressure deep within a glacier. That pressure squeezes out the tiny air bubbles that normally scatter all colors of light and make ice look white. Without those bubbles, the dense ice absorbs more red light than blue, so blue light is what reaches our eyes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Blue icebergs contain more air bubbles than white icebergs.","All icebergs are blue when they first break off from glaciers.","Icebergs appear white because they absorb blue light.","Some icebergs look blue because pressure removed air bubbles from their ice, changing how the ice interacts with light."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text explains how pressure removes bubbles, letting dense ice absorb red and show blue. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2e6cca698e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-42","passage":"<p>Historians studying a seventeenth-century merchant's account books noticed that his profits rose sharply in the years after he began recording every transaction, however small, in a single ledger. Earlier, he had kept separate notebooks for each ship and often lost track of debts owed to him. The historians suggest that the change in record keeping, rather than any change in the goods he traded, may explain his improved fortunes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Historians believe that account books from the period are unreliable.","The merchant lost several ships and had to rely on a single ledger.","Historians suggest that a merchant's profits may have risen because he adopted a more organized system of record keeping.","A seventeenth-century merchant began trading more valuable goods and became wealthy."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text connects the switch to a single ledger with rising profits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e9a54af11f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-43","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kettle hums before it sings, / a low and patient sound, / as if it knows the morning / needs a little time to come around. / And I, who rush from bed to door, / could stand to learn its art: / to warm up slowly, steadily, / before I make a start.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The speaker prefers to skip breakfast in order to leave early.","The speaker admires the kettle's gradual warming and wishes to approach the morning in the same unhurried way.","The speaker is frustrated that the kettle takes too long to boil.","The speaker describes how to repair a broken kettle."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The speaker \"could stand to learn its art: to warm up slowly.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7b318fbf4d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-44","passage":"<p>In many cities, rooftop gardens are praised for cooling buildings and reducing stormwater runoff. A research team measuring rooftops in one city confirmed both benefits but found a third that residents had not expected: the gardens attracted dozens of species of bees, including several rarely seen elsewhere in the city. The researchers now argue that rooftop gardens should be designed with pollinators in mind, using flowering plants that bloom at different times of year.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bees in the city prefer to nest on the ground rather than on rooftops.","Rooftop gardens should contain only plants that bloom in summer.","A study found that rooftop gardens also support bees, leading researchers to recommend designing the gardens to help pollinators.","Rooftop gardens do not actually cool buildings or reduce runoff."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The unexpected bee finding leads to the recommendation about pollinator-friendly design. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.03c5ab5f05","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-45","passage":"<p>For years, a small theater company performed classic plays in their original formal language. Attendance slowly declined. When a new director proposed staging the same plays with modern costumes and settings while keeping every original word, longtime members worried that the changes would distract from the text. Instead, audiences grew, and several reviewers wrote that the unfamiliar settings made them listen to the familiar words more closely than ever.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The theater company rewrote classic plays in modern language.","Reviewers criticized the new productions for distracting from the text.","Longtime members successfully prevented the director's changes.","Modern staging of classic plays, with the original words unchanged, attracted larger audiences and helped viewers engage with the text."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Audiences grew, and reviewers said the settings made them listen more closely. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.30e6116bbb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-46","passage":"<p>Hummingbirds have one of the highest metabolic rates of any animal and must eat almost constantly while awake. At night, however, many species enter a state called torpor, lowering their body temperature and heart rate dramatically. A hummingbird in torpor can use a small fraction of the energy it would need to stay fully active overnight, allowing it to survive until it can feed again in the morning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Hummingbirds eat throughout the night to maintain their energy.","Torpor causes hummingbirds to lose weight quickly.","Hummingbirds have unusually slow metabolisms.","Hummingbirds survive the night by entering torpor, which greatly reduces the energy they use."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text explains how torpor helps hummingbirds conserve energy overnight. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.48d977c78f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-47","passage":"<p>In the early twentieth century, a city built its main library with enormous windows on every side, at a time when most libraries relied on small windows and gas lamps. Critics complained that the glass would make the building cold and fade the books. The architect responded by placing reading tables near the windows and shelving the books in the building's interior, away from direct sunlight. The library's reading room became one of the most popular public spaces in the city.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Critics convinced the architect to remove the library's windows.","An architect designed a library with large windows and arranged its interior to address critics' concerns, creating a popular public space.","Most early twentieth-century libraries had very large windows.","The library's books were badly faded by sunlight."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The design, the criticism, the architect's arrangement, and the result. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9a7e6b832f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-51","passage":"<p>For centuries, residents of a desert region have built underground channels that carry water from distant mountain aquifers to their villages. Because the channels run beneath the surface, little water is lost to evaporation, even in extreme heat. Maintaining the channels requires workers to climb down vertical shafts to clear sand and repair walls, a skill traditionally passed from parents to children. As fewer young people learn the trade, some channels have fallen into disrepair.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Desert villages no longer need water from mountain aquifers.","Underground channels lose more water to evaporation than open canals.","Young people in the region have built many new channels.","Underground channels efficiently supply desert villages with water, but their future depends on passing down the skills needed to maintain them."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The text praises the channels' efficiency and notes the risk as fewer people learn maintenance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.15590b5932","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-52","passage":"<p>When a zoo redesigned its gorilla habitat, keepers replaced the concrete floor with soil, logs, and tall grasses and scattered food throughout the space instead of placing it in a single pile. Within months, the gorillas spent far more time exploring and foraging and far less time sitting idle. The keepers also observed fewer conflicts among the animals, perhaps because food was no longer concentrated in one spot that dominant gorillas could control.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The gorillas preferred their old concrete habitat.","Zoo keepers stopped feeding the gorillas after the redesign.","Dominant gorillas ate all the food in the new habitat.","A more natural habitat with scattered food made zoo gorillas more active and may have reduced conflicts among them."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The changes increased foraging and exploration and coincided with fewer conflicts. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ca41d9c50f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-54","passage":"<p>A city once required new buildings to include large parking garages, reasoning that plenty of parking would reduce traffic on crowded streets. Urban researchers later studied the policy's effects. They found that abundant, cheap parking encouraged more people to drive downtown, adding to congestion, while the garages took up space that could have been used for housing or shops. After reviewing the research, the city eliminated the requirement in its central district.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Building more parking garages reduced traffic congestion downtown.","Urban researchers recommended building larger parking garages.","The city required new buildings to include housing instead of parking.","Research showed that a city's parking requirement increased driving and used valuable space, leading the city to end the rule downtown."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The policy's reasoning, the research findings, and the city's decision. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.42aa1f1560","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-55","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Priya had practiced the violin piece for months, but in the recital hall her hands shook so badly that the first notes wavered. She thought of stopping. Then she noticed her grandfather in the third row, eyes closed, head tilted slightly, the way he listened to the radio on Sunday mornings. She drew the bow again, and this time the note held steady.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Priya has not practiced enough for her recital.","Priya's grandfather falls asleep during the recital.","Seeing her grandfather listen calmly helps Priya overcome her nervousness during a recital.","Priya decides to stop playing because she is too nervous."],"answer":"C","explanation":"After noticing him, \"this time the note held steady.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7e68708583","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-57","passage":"<p>Crows are known for their intelligence, but a study of city crows revealed a particularly surprising ability. Researchers wearing a distinctive mask trapped and released a few crows. For years afterward, crows in the area scolded and dive-bombed anyone wearing that mask, including crows that had never been trapped, while ignoring researchers in other masks. The birds had apparently learned to recognize a dangerous face and passed that knowledge to others.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Crows attack every person who wears a mask.","Only crows that had been trapped reacted to the mask.","Crows forget threatening experiences within a few days.","City crows learned to recognize a mask associated with danger and apparently shared that knowledge with other crows."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Even crows never trapped scolded the mask wearer for years. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.04bab61324","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-58","passage":"<p>In the 1950s, engineers designing a new highway through a valley proposed cutting down an old grove of oak trees. Local residents objected, noting that the grove was among the last remnants of the forest that once covered the valley. After months of debate, the engineers shifted the highway's route slightly to the east. The adjustment added a few hundred meters to the road, but today the grove is a protected park crossed by walking trails.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Engineers cut down the oak grove to build a shorter highway.","The valley's forest was fully preserved in the 1950s.","Residents asked engineers to build a highway through the grove.","Residents' objections led engineers to reroute a highway, preserving an old oak grove that is now a park."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The debate ended with a rerouted highway and a preserved grove. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.52ce9bcd53","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-59","passage":"<p>Children learning to read often sound out unfamiliar words letter by letter. Skilled adult readers, by contrast, recognize most familiar words instantly as whole units. Researchers studying eye movements have found, however, that even skilled readers slow down and fix their gaze longer on rare or unfamiliar words, suggesting that the older strategy of decoding piece by piece never disappears entirely but is used when needed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Eye movements are unrelated to reading skill.","Even skilled readers fall back on slower, piece-by-piece decoding when they encounter unfamiliar words.","Children recognize all words instantly as whole units.","Adults sound out every word they read."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Skilled readers slow down on unfamiliar words, showing the old strategy remains available. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.caf33a6357","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-60","passage":"<p>When a regional orchestra noticed that few young people attended its concerts, it began offering a free \"pay what you wish\" performance each month. Critics warned that people would come once for free and never buy full-price tickets. Two years of sales data showed a different pattern: many first-time attendees at the free concerts later returned to regular concerts, and the average age of subscribers dropped by nearly a decade.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["An orchestra's free monthly concerts attracted younger audiences who later returned for regular performances.","Older audiences stopped attending the orchestra's concerts.","The orchestra stopped charging for all of its concerts.","Critics were correct that free concerts would reduce ticket sales."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sales data showed returning attendees and younger subscribers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8fb650d394","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-64","passage":"<p>A school district tested a program in which high school students tutored younger children in reading twice a week. As expected, the younger children's reading scores improved. Less expected was the effect on the tutors: their own reading comprehension scores rose more than those of classmates who did not participate, and many reported feeling more confident speaking in class.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Only the younger children benefited from the tutoring program.","The program was canceled because reading scores fell.","A tutoring program helped not only the younger children being tutored but also the high school students who served as tutors.","High school tutors reported feeling less confident after the program."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Both groups improved, with an unexpected benefit for tutors. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a194ae2d5b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data3-65","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter had sat unopened on Tomas's desk for three days. He knew the university's decision was inside. Each morning he picked it up, weighed it in his hand as if its thickness could tell him the answer, and set it down again. On the fourth morning his little sister marched in, tore it open before he could stop her, and read the first line aloud with a shriek of delight.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Tomas opens the letter immediately and shares the news.","Tomas is too anxious to open a letter containing a university decision until his sister opens it and reveals good news.","Tomas throws away the letter without reading it.","Tomas's sister is disappointed by the university's decision."],"answer":"B","explanation":"He avoids opening it for days; his sister's \"shriek of delight\" suggests good news. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e63201d3c5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-69","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every Saturday, Rafael's grandmother made tamales, and every Saturday she refused to write down the recipe. \"It's in my hands,\" she would say, holding them up, dusted with masa. So Rafael began standing beside her, watching how much she scooped, how long she kneaded, and how she folded each husk. Years later, in his own kitchen, he realized that the recipe had become his hands too.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rafael dislikes cooking tamales with his grandmother.","Rafael forgets how to make tamales after his grandmother stops cooking.","By watching his grandmother closely over time, Rafael learns a family recipe that was never written down.","Rafael's grandmother writes down her recipe so that he can learn it."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He learns by watching, and the recipe \"had become his hands too.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f12701ed14","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-72","passage":"<p>Most spiders have eight eyes, but their eyesight varies widely. Web-building spiders often see poorly and sense prey mainly through vibrations in their silk. Jumping spiders, which hunt without webs, have large forward-facing eyes that give them some of the sharpest vision among small animals. This contrast suggests that a spider's hunting method is closely linked to how much it relies on sight.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Web-building spiders hunt by chasing prey across the ground.","All spiders have excellent eyesight.","Differences in spiders' eyesight appear to be tied to how they catch prey.","Jumping spiders build larger webs than other spiders."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Web builders rely on vibrations, and jumping spiders rely on sharp vision. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.64e138a765","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-73","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay about walking.</p><p>I used to measure a walk by its destination: the store, the office, the train. Then an injury took away my car for a season, and I began walking everywhere, slowly. I learned which porch held a sleeping cat, which corner smelled of bread at seven, which tree dropped its leaves first. The destinations were the same, but the walks had become places in themselves.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The writer's walks became shorter after the injury.","The writer moved to a new neighborhood after an injury.","Walking slowly out of necessity taught the writer to value the journey itself, not just the destination.","The writer dislikes walking and prefers driving."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The walks \"had become places in themselves.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.998184072e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-74","passage":"<p>Many people assume that the Great Pyramid's builders used slaves forced to work under harsh conditions. Excavations near the pyramid, however, have uncovered workers' villages with bakeries, breweries, and cemeteries where laborers were buried with bread and beer for the afterlife. Some skeletons show healed injuries that suggest the workers received medical care. These findings have led many archaeologists to conclude that the builders were paid laborers who were respected for their work.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The Great Pyramid was built entirely by slaves.","Archaeological evidence suggests that the Great Pyramid's builders were paid, respected workers rather than slaves.","Archaeologists have found no evidence about the pyramid's builders.","Workers near the pyramid had no access to food or medical care."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Villages, burials, and healed injuries support the paid-laborer view. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b7c4d149fa","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-76","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For weeks, Mei had avoided the old man who sold newspapers outside the station, embarrassed by her halting attempts at the language. One morning she was a coin short. Before she could apologize, he waved his hand, pressed the paper into hers, and pointed at a headline, slowly pronouncing each word. She repeated them after him. The next morning, she stopped at his stand on purpose.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The newspaper seller refuses to sell Mei a paper.","Mei stops buying newspapers because she cannot afford them.","Mei teaches the newspaper seller a new language.","An act of kindness from a newspaper seller helps Mei overcome her embarrassment about speaking a new language."],"answer":"D","explanation":"He helps her and practices words with her; she then visits \"on purpose.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.63b79951f7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-78","passage":"<p>When a large bookstore chain opened near a small independent bookshop, many predicted that the smaller store would close within a year. Instead, the owner began hosting weekly author readings, a children's story hour, and a book club that met in the shop after hours. Sales of new books dipped slightly, but sales of books connected to events rose, and the shop celebrated its twentieth anniversary a decade later.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The independent bookshop closed soon after the chain store opened.","An independent bookshop survived competition from a large chain by building a community around events.","The chain store began hosting author readings to compete.","The independent bookshop stopped selling new books."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Events built community and supported sales. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ab3fc39f81","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-81","passage":"<p>Roads built through forests can divide wildlife habitats, making it dangerous for animals to cross. In one mountain region, engineers built bridges covered with soil and native plants over a busy highway. Motion cameras showed deer, bears, and even small mammals using the crossings regularly. Collisions between vehicles and large animals on that stretch of highway dropped by more than eighty percent.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Collisions between vehicles and animals increased after the bridges were built.","Animals avoided the bridges built over the highway.","The highway was closed to protect wildlife.","Vegetated bridges over a highway allowed animals to cross safely and greatly reduced collisions with vehicles."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cameras showed frequent use, and collisions dropped sharply. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3866c647c2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-84","passage":"<p>Many schools ban phones in classrooms, arguing that the devices distract students. One district tested a policy requiring students to store phones in lockers during the school day. Over a year, teachers reported fewer interruptions and students' test scores rose slightly, with the largest gains among students who had previously struggled. Some students, however, reported feeling anxious without access to their phones, especially in the first weeks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A district's phone storage policy was linked to fewer interruptions and modest score gains, though some students initially felt anxious.","Banning phones caused test scores to fall sharply.","Students unanimously supported the phone policy.","Teachers reported more interruptions after the policy began."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Benefits and a drawback are both reported. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0a83ccf1b0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-85","passage":"<p>Paper was invented in China and spread gradually westward over many centuries. For much of that time, knowledge of papermaking was closely guarded. Historians note that papermaking reached new regions most often through trade routes and the movement of skilled workers, who brought their techniques with them. Once established, local paper mills made written records cheaper and far more common.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Paper was invented simultaneously in many regions.","Papermaking spread slowly through trade and skilled workers, and its arrival made written records more affordable and widespread.","Paper made written records more expensive and rare.","Papermaking techniques were freely shared from the beginning."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Spread via trade and workers; effects on records. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1842eac9f8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-86","passage":"<p>Mangrove trees grow in salty coastal water, where most plants cannot survive. Some mangroves filter salt out at their roots, while others absorb salt and then push it out through their leaves, which can be seen coated with white crystals. Mangrove forests also protect shorelines by slowing waves with their dense, tangled roots.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Mangroves cannot survive in salt water.","Mangroves have ways of coping with salt water and help protect coastlines.","Mangrove roots speed up waves along the shore.","All mangroves remove salt in exactly the same way."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text describes salt-handling methods and shoreline protection. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4ec106a1a7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-88","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The storm had passed, but the town was silent. No cars, no radios, no hum of refrigerators. Luis walked outside and heard things he had never noticed: water dripping from the gutter in a steady rhythm, a dog barking three streets away, his neighbor laughing on her porch. He stood there a long time, listening to a town he thought he knew.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The silence after a storm allows Luis to notice sounds in his town that he had never heard before.","Luis's neighbors are angry about the power outage.","Luis is frightened by the storm and stays inside.","Luis leaves town after the storm."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Without ordinary noise, he hears new sounds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0eaff86780","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-89","passage":"<p>Economists studying a coastal fishing town found that when the government set strict limits on how many fish each boat could catch, conflicts among fishers initially increased. Over time, however, fish populations recovered, catches became more predictable, and fishers' average incomes rose above their levels before the limits. Many fishers who had opposed the rules came to support them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Fish populations declined after the limits were introduced.","Catch limits in a fishing town caused early conflict but eventually led to recovered fish populations, more stable catches, and higher incomes.","Catch limits permanently reduced fishers' incomes.","Fishers unanimously supported the limits from the start."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The limits were unpopular at first but produced long-term benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b46ae3dd4d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data4-90","passage":"<p>Some ancient Roman concrete structures, such as harbor walls, have survived in seawater for nearly two thousand years, while many modern concrete structures crack within decades. Researchers analyzing samples found that seawater seeping into the Roman concrete reacted with volcanic ash in the mixture, forming new minerals that filled cracks and made the material stronger over time.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Modern concrete lasts longer in seawater than Roman concrete.","Roman concrete has lasted in seawater partly because reactions with volcanic ash strengthened it over time.","Seawater weakens all types of concrete.","Roman builders avoided building near the sea."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Seawater plus volcanic ash formed crack-filling minerals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7870382d50","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-96","passage":"<p>When a remote island installed solar panels and batteries to replace its diesel generators, officials worried that cloudy weeks would leave residents without power. The system's designers addressed the concern by oversizing the battery bank and keeping one small generator for emergencies. In the first three years, the generator ran for a total of only eleven days, and the island's fuel costs fell by more than ninety percent.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["An island's switch to solar power with large batteries proved reliable and dramatically reduced fuel costs.","The island removed all of its generators.","The island's solar system failed during cloudy weeks.","Fuel costs on the island rose after the switch to solar power."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The backup generator was rarely needed, and fuel costs fell sharply. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.de18845306","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-97","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Aunt Rosa's kitchen radio had been broken for years, stuck forever on a station that played old dance music. Everyone offered to buy her a new one. She refused. When I asked her why, she turned up the volume, took my hands, and spun me across the linoleum floor, laughing, until the song ended. \"Now,\" she said, slightly out of breath, \"you tell me what's broken.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Aunt Rosa asks the narrator to fix her old radio.","Aunt Rosa shows the narrator that the \"broken\" radio still brings her joy.","The narrator buys Aunt Rosa a new radio.","Aunt Rosa dislikes dance music."],"answer":"B","explanation":"By dancing to the music, she shows that nothing important is broken. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0d73c133ab","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-105","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The chess club had lost every match that season. At the final tournament, twelve-year-old Imani faced the league's top player, a high school senior who had not lost a game in two years. Forty minutes in, the senior frowned, studied the board for a long time, and then extended his hand across the table. The room went silent. Imani stared at his hand before she understood.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The high school senior refuses to shake Imani's hand.","Imani, a young player from a struggling club, wins a game against the league's top player.","Imani loses her match to the league's top player.","The chess club wins every match of the season."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The undefeated senior extends his hand, conceding the game. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cb9261d5e2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-106","passage":"<p>When a river city experienced repeated floods, planners considered building taller levees. Instead, they converted a strip of low-lying land along the river into a park designed to flood safely, with grassy fields and paths that could be submerged without serious damage. During heavy rains, the park absorbs overflow that would otherwise reach nearby streets. In dry months, residents use it for sports and picnics.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The park is closed to residents throughout the year.","Heavy rains no longer occur in the city.","The city built taller levees along the river.","A city reduced flooding by turning riverside land into a park that can safely hold floodwater and serve residents in dry months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The floodable park absorbs overflow and serves as recreation space. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6206d6b5cc","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-107","passage":"<p>Some cities have experimented with replacing traffic signals at busy intersections with roundabouts. At signals, cars sometimes speed through yellow lights, and the most dangerous crashes happen when vehicles collide at right angles. In roundabouts, all traffic moves in the same direction at lower speeds, so collisions that do occur tend to be glancing blows rather than direct hits. Studies of converted intersections have found large drops in serious injuries.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Cities have removed all roundabouts.","Drivers speed up when entering roundabouts.","Roundabouts cause more serious crashes than traffic signals.","Roundabouts can reduce serious injuries because traffic moves slowly in one direction, making severe collisions less likely."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Lower speeds and same-direction traffic reduce severe crashes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7580fb65ca","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-110","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Wen had moved to the city expecting noise, crowds, and strangers who never looked at one another. What surprised her was the bakery on the corner. The owner learned her name on her second visit, set aside a sesame roll for her each morning, and asked about her new job as though they had known each other for years. By winter, the corner no longer felt like part of a strange city. It felt like her street.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Wen opens a bakery after moving to the city.","A friendly bakery owner helps Wen feel at home in an unfamiliar city.","Wen decides to return to her hometown by winter.","Wen finds the city exactly as unfriendly as she expected."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The owner's warmth makes the corner feel like \"her street.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.54561c6333","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-111","passage":"<p>In the early years of aviation, many engineers tried to build flying machines that flapped their wings like birds. These designs rarely worked. Later inventors achieved sustained flight by separating two functions that birds' wings combine: fixed wings provided lift, while propellers provided forward thrust. Studying birds was useful, but copying them too literally had led engineers astray.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Successful early aircraft separated lift and thrust instead of directly imitating birds' flapping wings.","Early engineers built successful aircraft with flapping wings.","Birds do not use their wings for lift.","Propellers provide lift, while wings provide thrust."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Fixed wings and propellers replaced flapping designs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.444544e150","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-112","passage":"<p>Researchers tracking city foxes with GPS collars found that the animals adjusted their routines to avoid people. Foxes that lived near busy parks were active mainly after midnight, while foxes in quieter suburbs often moved about in the early evening. The researchers noted that the foxes' diets also differed, with park foxes eating more discarded human food.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["City foxes adjusted when they were active depending on how busy their surroundings were, and their diets varied as well.","Foxes in all parts of the city followed identical schedules.","Foxes near busy parks were active mainly in the early evening.","City foxes avoid eating human food."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Activity timing and diet differed by location. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4d60e48511","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-118","passage":"<p>A city parks department noticed that its public benches were rarely used. Designers observed that most benches faced empty lawns or walls. They moved several benches to face walkways, playgrounds, and a fountain, where people could watch activity. Within weeks, the relocated benches were among the most used in the park, while those left in place remained mostly empty.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Moving park benches to face activity greatly increased how often people used them.","Benches were used equally regardless of their position.","People prefer benches facing empty lawns.","The parks department removed all of its benches."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Relocated benches facing activity became popular. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.16d7216f67","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-121","passage":"<p>Some schools have replaced traditional letter grades with detailed written feedback. Supporters argue that grades encourage students to focus on scores rather than learning. In one study, students who received only comments on their essays improved more on later assignments than students who received grades alone. Students who received both grades and comments, however, improved about as little as those who received grades alone, apparently paying attention mainly to the grade.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A study found that essay comments helped students improve most when they were not accompanied by grades.","Students improved most when they received both grades and comments.","Grades alone produced the largest improvement.","Written comments had no effect on student writing."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Comments alone led to the most improvement; grades seemed to draw attention away. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9c2f5d35fd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-122","passage":"<p>Coastal fog is an important water source for some redwood forests. Scientists measuring moisture found that during dry summers, redwoods absorb water from fog through their leaves, and fog dripping from branches can supply a substantial share of the water reaching the forest floor. Researchers are concerned that warming ocean temperatures could reduce fog along the coast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Redwoods receive all of their water from rainfall.","Redwood forests rely partly on fog for water, so reductions in coastal fog could threaten them.","Fog harms redwood leaves.","Warming oceans will increase fog along the coast."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Fog supplies summer moisture; less fog is a concern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b3c5595f98","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data5-123","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The science fair judges had moved on, and Dario's volcano had not erupted. He had checked the vinegar and the baking soda twice. His friends drifted away to watch a robot that played music. Alone at his table, Dario lifted the lid of the volcano and saw it: a folded note from his little sister, taped inside, blocking the tube. It read, \"Good luck!!!\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Dario's volcano erupts in front of the judges.","Dario discovers that his volcano failed because his sister's well-meaning note was blocking it.","Dario's sister builds a robot for the science fair.","Dario's friends sabotage his project."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The \"Good luck\" note blocked the tube. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.506b72991a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-125","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather had kept bees for fifty years, and he spoke to them every morning. \"Good morning, ladies,\" he would say, lifting the lid. My mother said it was nonsense. But the summer he was in the hospital and she tended the hives, I heard her through the kitchen window, standing by the boxes in her bathrobe, saying softly, \"Good morning, ladies. He'll be home soon.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator teaches the bees to respond to speech.","The narrator's mother refuses to care for the bees.","The grandfather stops keeping bees after fifty years.","The narrator's mother, who once dismissed her father's habit, adopts it while he is in the hospital."],"answer":"D","explanation":"She repeats his greeting while he is away. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a9417b97a4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-126","passage":"<p>Scientists studying bamboo noticed that many species flower only once every several decades, and when they do, nearly all plants of the species flower at the same time, even in different countries. After flowering, the plants die. The simultaneous flowering produces an enormous quantity of seeds. Researchers suggest that this strategy may overwhelm seed-eating animals, which cannot eat all the seeds, allowing many to sprout.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bamboo flowers every year in small numbers.","Bamboo plants survive for decades after flowering.","Some bamboo species flower all at once after decades, possibly producing more seeds than animals can eat.","Seed-eating animals prevent bamboo from spreading."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Mass flowering may overwhelm seed predators. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.02fa1cafd8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-131","passage":"<p>Many species of songbird learn their songs by listening to adults during a sensitive period early in life. Researchers raising young birds in soundproof rooms found that birds that never heard adult songs developed simple, unusual songs. Birds that heard recordings of their own species during the sensitive period developed normal songs, while those exposed only after the period ended did not.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Songbirds sing normal songs without ever hearing other birds.","Recordings have no effect on songbird learning.","Adult songbirds learn new songs every year.","Young songbirds must hear adult songs during an early sensitive period to develop normal songs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Timing of exposure determined whether songs developed normally. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3a7aaaa324","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-132","passage":"<p>Some libraries have eliminated late fees. Supporters argue that fees discourage low-income patrons, who may stop borrowing books rather than risk charges. One library system that removed fees found that many cardholders who had been blocked because of unpaid fines returned, and the rate at which books came back on time barely changed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Late fees encourage more people to use libraries.","Removing late fees caused most books to be returned late.","Removing late fees brought back many patrons without significantly affecting how promptly books were returned.","Libraries raised fees to improve return rates."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Blocked patrons returned, and on-time returns barely changed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ca11931d19","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-133","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Ana had always hated her middle name, Esperanza, which the other kids pronounced wrong on purpose. At her grandmother's funeral, the priest explained that Esperanza meant \"hope,\" and that her grandmother had chosen it during a hard winter when the family had very little. Walking out of the church, Ana said the name quietly to herself, trying it out as if for the first time.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ana's grandmother never liked the name Esperanza.","Ana's classmates begin pronouncing her name correctly.","Learning the meaning and history of her middle name changes how Ana feels about it.","Ana decides to change her middle name."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The name's meaning and origin lead her to reconsider it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e2815d71be","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-134","passage":"<p>Researchers studying ants noticed that when a colony's usual path to food was blocked, the ants quickly found a new route. Individual ants wandered somewhat randomly, but those that found food laid down chemical trails on the way back. Shorter routes were traveled more often, so their trails grew stronger, attracting more ants. In time, most ants followed the shortest available path, even though no single ant planned it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Blocked paths prevent ants from finding food.","A leader ant chooses the best route for the colony.","Ant colonies find short routes through chemical trails that strengthen with use, without any individual planning the route.","Ants avoid paths that other ants have used."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Stronger trails on shorter routes lead most ants to them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.27df710f36","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-135","passage":"<p>In the nineteenth century, many people believed that tomatoes, a member of the nightshade family, were poisonous. Some nightshade plants are indeed toxic, and tomato leaves contain small amounts of harmful compounds. The fruit itself, however, is safe to eat. As more people cooked with tomatoes and suffered no ill effects, the fear faded, and tomatoes became a staple in many cuisines.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Tomato fruits are poisonous to eat.","All nightshade plants are safe.","Fears that tomatoes were poisonous faded as people ate the fruit safely, and tomatoes became widely used.","Tomatoes were never eaten in the nineteenth century."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The misconception had some basis but faded with experience. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1ca9d809bd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-138","passage":"<p>Scientists investigating why some people get more mosquito bites than others found that mosquitoes are attracted partly by carbon dioxide and certain skin chemicals. People who produced more of particular fatty acids on their skin attracted mosquitoes far more strongly in lab tests, and this preference remained consistent when the same people were tested months apart.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Mosquito preferences change from week to week.","Carbon dioxide repels mosquitoes.","Mosquitoes bite everyone equally.","Mosquitoes are attracted to some people more than others partly because of consistent differences in skin chemicals."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Skin fatty acids predicted consistent attraction. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.4035ef91d2","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-139","passage":"<p>For much of the twentieth century, doctors advised patients with back pain to rest in bed. Later studies found that patients who stayed active, within limits, often recovered faster than those who rested. Many guidelines now recommend gentle movement, such as walking, for most cases of short-term back pain.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Walking always worsens back pain.","Research has shifted medical advice for back pain from bed rest toward staying gently active.","Doctors have never recommended bed rest.","Bed rest is the best treatment for all back pain."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Studies showing faster recovery with activity changed guidelines. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7ab408adb8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-140","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient village, they found that one house contained far more imported pottery, jewelry, and fine tools than the others. Some suggested that a wealthy chief lived there. Others noted that the house was also unusually large and contained many storage pits, and proposed that it may have been a communal building where valuable goods were kept for the whole village.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Archaeologists disagree about whether a house full of valuable goods belonged to a wealthy leader or served as a communal storage building.","Every house in the village contained the same objects.","The village had no imported goods.","Archaeologists agree that a chief lived in the largest house."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Two interpretations of the same evidence are presented. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c3634ce1ad","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-141","passage":"<p>Some city governments have begun painting large murals on the walls of buildings in neighborhoods with high rates of graffiti. In several cities, murals were rarely tagged with graffiti, while plain walls nearby continued to be marked. Observers suggest that graffiti writers may respect the murals as art, or that the busy designs leave little blank space that invites tagging.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Cities stopped painting murals because they were tagged.","Murals appear to discourage graffiti, possibly because of respect for the art or a lack of blank space.","Murals attract more graffiti than plain walls.","Graffiti writers prefer painting on murals."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Murals were rarely tagged; two explanations are offered. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.9425adfde4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-143","passage":"<p>Many people assume that pandas eat bamboo because it is highly nutritious. In fact, bamboo is low in nutrients, and pandas digest only a small portion of what they eat. To survive, they eat for up to fourteen hours a day and conserve energy by moving relatively little. Their low-energy lifestyle is an adaptation to a diet that provides little fuel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bamboo is the most nutritious food available to pandas.","Pandas survive on nutrient-poor bamboo by eating large amounts and conserving energy.","Pandas digest nearly all of the bamboo they eat.","Pandas are extremely active animals."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Low nutrition leads to long feeding and low activity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.aa47c6bf3f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-145","passage":"<p>When a coastal town was hit by a severe storm, residents who lived near a restored salt marsh reported less flooding than those living near a stretch of shoreline protected only by a concrete wall. Scientists noted that the marsh's plants slowed incoming waves and absorbed water, and that, unlike the wall, the marsh had not been damaged by the storm.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A restored salt marsh reduced flooding during a storm and withstood the storm better than a concrete wall.","The concrete wall prevented more flooding than the marsh.","Residents near the marsh experienced more flooding.","The marsh was destroyed by the storm."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Less flooding near the marsh; the marsh was undamaged. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d5ed3e5938","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data6-147","passage":"<p>Engineers designing a new stadium roof studied the structure of bird bones, which are hollow but reinforced with thin internal struts. The design allows bones to be light yet strong. Using a similar approach, the engineers created roof beams with hollow interiors crossed by thin supports, reducing the roof's weight by nearly a third without sacrificing strength.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The stadium roof was made heavier to increase strength.","Engineers used the internal structure of bird bones as a model to create lighter, strong roof beams.","Bird bones are solid and heavy.","Engineers replaced the roof with bird feathers."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Hollow, strutted beams mimic bird bones. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6a7d2f9554","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-151","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every photograph in the hallway showed Grandmother smiling, but Lena had never once seen her smile in person. At the funeral, an old woman Lena did not recognize took her hand and said, \"Your grandmother laughed louder than anyone I ever knew, before the war.\" Lena looked back at the hallway photographs and noticed, for the first time, that all of them had been taken long ago.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The old woman had never met Lena's grandmother.","Lena's grandmother smiled frequently in Lena's presence.","The hallway photographs were taken recently.","Lena learns that her grandmother was once joyful and realizes that the smiling photographs come from an earlier time in her life."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The stranger's comment and the old photographs reveal her grandmother's past. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e91928f996","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-153","passage":"<p>A city that installed sensors to monitor noise levels discovered that the loudest hours in one neighborhood were not late at night, as residents' complaints had suggested, but early in the morning, when delivery trucks unloaded at restaurants. Officials worked with businesses to shift deliveries to midday, and noise complaints fell.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Noise sensors revealed that early-morning deliveries were a major source of noise, and rescheduling them reduced complaints.","Late-night noise was the main problem in the neighborhood.","Restaurants stopped receiving deliveries.","Residents stopped complaining after the sensors were installed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sensors identified the true peak, leading to schedule changes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a685cb1324","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-154","passage":"<p>For years, scientists debated whether some dinosaurs cared for their young. Discoveries of fossilized nests changed the conversation. At one site, adult skeletons were found sitting on clutches of eggs in positions resembling those of brooding birds, suggesting that the adults were protecting or warming the eggs when they died.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Dinosaurs abandoned their eggs immediately after laying them.","Fossils of adults positioned over eggs suggest that some dinosaurs cared for their eggs.","Scientists have never found dinosaur nests.","Brooding birds are descended from dinosaurs that ate eggs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Adults on nests resemble brooding birds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.65235163d0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-155","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>The first time I saw the ocean, I was sixteen and disappointed. I had imagined something like the postcards: impossible blue, gentle waves. The real ocean that day was gray and loud and cold, and it smelled of salt and seaweed. It took me years to understand that I had not been disappointed by the ocean. I had been disappointed by the postcards.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The writer eventually realizes that her disappointment came from unrealistic expectations rather than from the ocean itself.","The writer still dislikes the ocean.","The writer's first visit to the ocean matched her expectations exactly.","The writer collects postcards of the ocean."],"answer":"A","explanation":"She blames the postcards' idealized image. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6cef352158","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-156","passage":"<p>When a school introduced a program in which students grew vegetables in a garden and then cooked them in class, researchers surveyed students' eating habits before and after. Students in the program reported eating more vegetables at home, and many said they were more willing to try unfamiliar foods. Students at a similar school without the program showed no such change.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A school gardening and cooking program was linked to students eating more vegetables and trying new foods.","The comparison school also saw large changes in eating habits.","Students in the program ate fewer vegetables.","Students refused to cook the vegetables they grew."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The program students improved; the comparison school did not. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2fcc5389a4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-157","passage":"<p>Ancient Polynesian navigators crossed vast stretches of the Pacific without instruments. They read the positions of stars, the direction and shape of ocean swells, and the flight paths of seabirds, which head out to sea in the morning and return to land at dusk. By combining these clues, navigators could locate islands far beyond the horizon.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Navigators could not travel beyond sight of land.","Seabirds always fly away from land at dusk.","Polynesian navigators relied on modern instruments.","Polynesian navigators found distant islands by combining clues from stars, swells, and seabirds."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Multiple natural clues guided voyages. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1a744f9768","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-159","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The lighthouse keeper had not spoken to another person in three weeks when the supply boat arrived. He had rehearsed what he would say: news about the storms, a request for more lamp oil. But when the young sailor jumped onto the dock and said hello, the keeper found that all he wanted was to hear the boy talk, about anything at all.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The sailor brings news that the lighthouse will close.","The lighthouse keeper refuses to speak to the sailor.","The keeper forgets to request lamp oil because he is angry.","After weeks alone, the lighthouse keeper discovers that he longs simply for conversation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"He wants \"to hear the boy talk, about anything at all.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.739c3d4f00","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-160","passage":"<p>A tech company tested two versions of a sign-up page. One asked visitors for their name, email, phone number, and address. The other asked only for an email address. The shorter form had nearly twice as many sign-ups. The company concluded that each additional request for information discourages some visitors from completing the process.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A sign-up page that requested less information attracted far more sign-ups.","Visitors preferred longer sign-up forms.","The company removed its sign-up page.","Asking for phone numbers increased sign-ups."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The shorter form nearly doubled sign-ups. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.10930b557e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-162","passage":"<p>When a museum added benches to its galleries, curators noticed that visitors spent more time looking at individual artworks. Before, most people walked past paintings quickly while standing. With a place to sit, visitors often stayed in front of a single piece for several minutes, and some reported noticing details they would otherwise have missed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Visitors spent less time in galleries after benches were added.","Visitors preferred standing while viewing art.","Benches were removed because visitors complained.","Adding benches in galleries led museum visitors to spend more time looking closely at artworks."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sitting encouraged longer, closer viewing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.dd051b558d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-166","passage":"<p>Linguists recording conversations noticed that speakers often pause briefly before important or unexpected words. Listeners, in experiments, were better at remembering words that followed such pauses. The researchers suggest that pauses may help listeners by signaling that something worth attention is coming.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Brief pauses before important words may help listeners remember those words by signaling their significance.","Pauses make speech harder to understand.","Speakers never pause during conversations.","Listeners remember words that come before pauses best."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Words after pauses were remembered better. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7e28389d27","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-167","passage":"<p>For decades, a small town's annual fair featured a pie contest judged by the same three residents. When the organizers began inviting judges from neighboring towns, several longtime participants worried that outsiders would not appreciate local recipes. Instead, entries increased, and a pie made with a rarely used local fruit won first prize for the first time, drawing new attention to the fruit.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The pie contest ended after outside judges were invited.","Local recipes were ignored by the new judges.","Longtime participants stopped entering the contest.","Bringing in outside judges increased participation and led to recognition of a local fruit in a town's pie contest."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Entries rose, and a local-fruit pie won. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1e978427be","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-168","passage":"<p>Scientists studying why zebras have stripes tested several ideas. When horses wore striped coats, biting flies landed on them far less often than on horses wearing solid-colored coats, although flies still approached. The researchers suggest that stripes may confuse flies as they try to land, which could help zebras avoid insect-borne diseases.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Stripes attract biting flies.","Horses in striped coats were bitten more often.","Flies cannot see striped patterns.","Experiments suggest that stripes may help protect animals from biting flies by making landing difficult."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Flies approached but landed less on stripes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6946a5bf90","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-170","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The old bridge knows the river's moods: / the spring flood's rushing rage, / the summer's lazy silver hum, / the winter's frozen stage. / It does not fight the water, / it simply holds its ground, / and lets each season pass beneath / without a single sound.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The poem describes a bridge that endures the river's changing seasons calmly and steadily.","The speaker complains about the river's noise.","The poem compares two bridges over the same river.","The poem describes a bridge destroyed by a flood."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The bridge \"holds its ground\" through every season. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8413777330","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-171","passage":"<p>Researchers studying how people read food labels used eye-tracking devices and found that most shoppers looked at the calorie count and the price but rarely read the ingredient list. When the ingredient list was moved to the front of the package and printed in larger type, shoppers were far more likely to read it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Moving ingredient lists reduced how often they were read.","Shoppers rarely read ingredient lists unless the lists are made more prominent on packages.","Shoppers always read ingredient lists carefully.","Shoppers ignore calorie counts."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Prominent placement increased reading. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1dc8a19fbf","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data7-175","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Marco had lived above the bakery for ten years and had long since stopped noticing the smell of bread. Then he spent a month traveling for work. On the night he returned, he climbed the stairs, opened his door, and stopped, overwhelmed by the warm scent of yeast and butter drifting up through the floorboards, as though the building itself were welcoming him home.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Marco moves away from the bakery permanently.","The bakery closed while Marco was traveling.","Marco dislikes the smell of bread.","After being away, Marco notices again the familiar smell of bread that makes his home feel welcoming."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Time away renews his awareness of the scent. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.34638ecdff","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-177","passage":"<p>When a city began offering free swimming lessons to children in neighborhoods without public pools, officials bused students to a recreation center across town. Participation was lower than expected. Surveys found that many families could not accommodate the long bus rides after school. The city then set up temporary pools at neighborhood schools during the summer, and enrollment tripled.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The city closed its recreation center.","Children preferred long bus rides to swimming lessons.","Families were uninterested in swimming lessons.","A free swimming program attracted far more children once lessons were moved closer to their neighborhoods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Moving lessons to neighborhood schools tripled enrollment. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.82a8bae106","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-178","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The old clock in the hallway had stopped at 4:17 the day Grandfather died, and no one had wound it since. Years passed. Then, on the morning of her own wedding, Tessa found her father standing in front of it with the key in his hand. He wound it slowly, and as the pendulum began to swing, he said, without turning around, \"It's time.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Tessa's father throws away the old clock.","On his daughter's wedding day, Tessa's father restarts a clock that had stopped when his own father died, marking a new beginning.","Tessa winds the clock to remember her grandfather.","The clock breaks on the day of Tessa's wedding."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Winding the clock marks moving forward. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.869eaa599e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-179","passage":"<p>Researchers studying how people navigate cities found that residents who relied heavily on GPS directions formed less accurate mental maps of their neighborhoods than residents who navigated without it. When asked to draw maps or estimate distances between landmarks, GPS users made more errors, though they reached destinations just as reliably.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Relying on GPS was linked to less accurate mental maps, even though GPS users reached destinations reliably.","Residents who navigated without GPS could not reach destinations.","GPS users got lost more often than other residents.","GPS improves people's mental maps."],"answer":"A","explanation":"GPS users made more mapping errors but arrived reliably. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2052bf7925","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-181","passage":"<p>When a hospital began having pharmacists review patients' medication lists at discharge, errors in prescriptions sent home with patients dropped sharply. Pharmacists often caught medications that duplicated one another or could interact harmfully, problems that busy doctors had missed. Readmissions related to medication problems also declined.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Readmissions increased after the change.","Adding pharmacist review at discharge reduced medication errors and related readmissions.","Doctors never make medication errors.","Pharmacists caused more prescription errors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Pharmacists caught duplications and interactions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.872f31f5f8","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-182","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>As a child I believed that adults knew exactly what they were doing. My parents paid bills, fixed leaks, and made decisions with what looked like total confidence. It was only when I became an adult myself that I realized how much of that confidence was performance, a steady face kept up so that children would feel safe while the grown-ups figured things out.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The writer realizes as an adult that her parents' apparent confidence often masked uncertainty meant to reassure their children.","The writer's parents were unable to fix leaks.","The writer's parents never paid bills.","The writer still believes adults always know what they are doing."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Confidence was partly \"performance.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.af5d100b13","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-183","passage":"<p>Ecologists found that frogs in ponds near busy roads call at higher pitches than frogs of the same species in quiet ponds. Traffic noise is concentrated at low frequencies, and higher-pitched calls may be easier for other frogs to hear over that noise. However, females of the species generally prefer lower-pitched calls, which typically come from larger males.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Frogs near roads call at higher pitches, which may help them be heard over traffic but could make their calls less attractive to females.","Traffic noise is concentrated at high frequencies.","Females prefer higher-pitched calls.","Frogs near roads call at lower pitches."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Higher pitch aids audibility but conflicts with female preference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.cb6bc1ed2a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-184","passage":"<p>A company that produces breakfast cereal tested whether placing a cartoon character on the box affected children's choices. In a taste test, children were given the same cereal in two boxes, one with a character and one without. Most children said the cereal from the character box tasted better, even though the cereal was identical.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The cereals in the two boxes tasted different.","Cartoon characters improve the nutrition of cereal.","Children preferred boxes without characters.","Children rated identical cereal as tastier when it came from a box featuring a cartoon character."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Packaging influenced perceived taste. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b4d6412954","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-185","passage":"<p>When scientists first proposed that the continents slowly move, many geologists objected because no one could explain what force could push such enormous masses. Decades later, maps of the ocean floor revealed long underwater mountain ranges where new crust forms and spreads outward. This discovery provided a mechanism, and the idea of moving continents gained broad acceptance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ocean floor maps disproved continental movement.","The discovery of seafloor spreading supplied a mechanism that led to acceptance of the idea that continents move.","Geologists immediately accepted the idea that continents move.","No underwater mountain ranges exist."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A mechanism was needed and later found. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.aaa4b6ad14","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-186","passage":"<p>A school district that shortened its summer break and added more short breaks throughout the year hoped to reduce learning loss. Teachers reported spending less time reviewing material in the fall. Some families, however, struggled to find child care during the new breaks, which did not align with the schedules of nearby districts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["All families found the new schedule convenient.","Teachers spent more time reviewing material.","The new calendar increased summer learning loss.","A revised school calendar reduced review time for teachers but created child care challenges for some families."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benefits for teaching, drawbacks for child care. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d01c7a61b6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-187","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Kenji's father spoke little English and rarely came to school events. So when Kenji saw him standing at the back of the auditorium during the science fair, still in his work uniform, Kenji nearly dropped his poster. His father did not ask questions or read the display. He simply stood beside it for the entire evening, nodding at every visitor who stopped.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Kenji's father shows his support by attending the science fair and staying beside Kenji's project all evening.","Kenji is embarrassed that his father attends.","Kenji's father refuses to attend the science fair.","Kenji's father explains the project to every visitor."],"answer":"A","explanation":"His quiet presence expresses support. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3b1c4dd775","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-191","passage":"<p>When a small town's only doctor retired, residents feared they would lose local medical care. The town partnered with a nearby hospital to set up video appointments at the library, staffed by a nurse who could take measurements during calls. Many residents were initially skeptical, but within a year most routine visits happened this way, and patients reported saving hours of travel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Residents refused to use video appointments.","The library stopped hosting appointments.","After losing its doctor, a town used library-based video appointments that became widely used and saved patients travel time.","The town hired a new doctor immediately."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Video visits became common and convenient. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.61fc74b35a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data8-192","passage":"<p>Researchers analyzing thousands of job postings found that ads using words such as \"competitive\" and \"dominant\" attracted fewer applications from women than ads describing the same jobs with words such as \"collaborative\" and \"supportive.\" When a company revised its ads to use more neutral language, the share of women applying increased.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The wording of job ads appeared to influence how many women applied, and neutral wording increased applications from women.","Revised ads reduced applications from women.","Job postings rarely use descriptive words.","Women applied equally regardless of ad wording."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Wording affected who applied. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f49472ad4e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-198","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter from her brother arrived on the first day of spring, three years after their last argument. Mira read the single line twice—\"I planted the tomatoes the way you always said\"—and then sat down on the porch step and laughed until she cried. Their fight had been about something far larger than tomatoes, but she understood exactly what he meant.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A brief, seemingly ordinary message from her brother signals reconciliation to Mira after a long estrangement.","Mira and her brother argue about gardening.","Mira's brother writes to explain how to plant tomatoes.","Mira refuses to read her brother's letter."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The line about tomatoes is a gesture of peace. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.acf4dfbb37","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-200","passage":"<p>In the early twentieth century, many cities replaced horse-drawn carriages with automobiles, partly to solve a pollution problem: horse manure filled streets, attracting flies and spreading disease. Cars eliminated that problem but introduced new ones, including exhaust fumes and traffic accidents, that later generations would struggle to address.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Cities returned to using horses.","Automobiles solved the problem of horse manure in cities but created new problems of their own.","Horse-drawn carriages caused no pollution.","Cars had no negative effects on cities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"One solution produced new problems. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d2da4e43b9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-202","passage":"<p>When scientists studied honey found in ancient tombs, they discovered that some of it was still edible after thousands of years. Honey's low water content and high acidity make it inhospitable to bacteria, and bees add an enzyme that produces small amounts of hydrogen peroxide, which further prevents spoilage.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Honey spoils quickly without refrigeration.","Honey resists spoiling for very long periods because of its low moisture, acidity, and natural antibacterial compounds.","Bees add water to honey to preserve it.","Ancient honey was unsafe to eat."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Its chemistry prevents bacterial growth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.df412d196d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-204","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My first apartment had a window that looked directly at a brick wall. For a year I resented it. Then one spring a pair of sparrows built a nest in a gap between the bricks, and I found myself waking early to watch them carry grass and string. By the time the chicks fledged, I had stopped thinking of it as a bad view.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator removes the sparrows' nest.","A pair of nesting sparrows changes the narrator's feelings about a view she had resented.","The narrator's window faces a park.","The narrator moves to a new apartment with a better view."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Watching the birds transforms her view. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a2c9eb9d2d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-205","passage":"<p>Astronomers studying a distant star noticed that its brightness dipped irregularly and by unusually large amounts, unlike the small, regular dips caused by orbiting planets. Some proposed that swarms of comets or clouds of dust were passing in front of it. Later observations showing that the dimming was stronger in blue light than red light supported the dust explanation, since fine dust blocks blue light more.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Dust blocks red light more than blue light.","The star's brightness never changed.","Observations of how a star's dimming varied by color supported the idea that dust caused its irregular dimming.","The star dimmed because of a single orbiting planet."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Color-dependent dimming pointed to dust. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6138b55d97","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-207","passage":"<p>Some trees in dense forests grow tall and thin, while trees of the same species growing in open fields spread wide branches and remain shorter. In a crowded forest, sunlight is limited at lower levels, so growing upward quickly helps a tree reach light. In an open field, sunlight is available all around, and wide branches can capture it efficiently.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Trees in open fields grow taller than forest trees.","Sunlight is abundant at low levels in dense forests.","Trees in forests are a different species from trees in fields.","Trees of the same species grow differently depending on how much light is available in their surroundings."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Shape reflects light availability. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c0637e43a6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-211","passage":"<p>Biologists noticed that some species of butterfly that are harmless to birds closely resemble species that taste bitter or are toxic. Birds that have learned to avoid the toxic butterflies also tend to avoid the harmless look-alikes. The resemblance, researchers suggest, protects the harmless species even though it has no defenses of its own.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Toxic butterflies imitate harmless ones.","Harmless butterflies taste bitter to birds.","Harmless butterflies that resemble toxic species are protected because birds avoid them too.","Birds prefer to eat toxic butterflies."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Resemblance to toxic species deters birds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f75d2b37f7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-213","passage":"<p>When a bakery began selling day-old bread at half price, the owner expected sales of fresh bread to fall. Instead, fresh bread sales held steady, and the discounted bread attracted new customers, some of whom later bought fresh items. Food waste at the bakery also dropped.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Customers refused to buy day-old bread.","Fresh bread sales fell sharply after the discount.","Selling day-old bread at a discount attracted new customers and reduced waste without hurting fresh bread sales.","The bakery stopped baking fresh bread."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Steady sales, new customers, and less waste. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bce2b7c137","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-214","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>I kept my grandmother's thimble / in a drawer for twenty years, / a silver cap too small / for any finger but her own. / Tonight I sewed a button / on my daughter's winter coat, / and without thinking, / I slipped the thimble on.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The thimble fits the daughter perfectly.","The speaker throws away her grandmother's thimble.","The speaker's grandmother sews a coat.","The speaker unconsciously uses her grandmother's thimble while sewing, linking three generations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The thimble connects grandmother, speaker, and daughter. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bbe8186769","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data9-215","passage":"<p>A city testing electric buses found that they cost more to buy than diesel buses but less to fuel and maintain. Over a bus's expected lifetime, the total costs were roughly equal. The electric buses also reduced noise and exhaust along routes through dense neighborhoods, which residents reported appreciating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Electric buses cost more upfront but had similar lifetime costs to diesel buses and reduced noise and exhaust.","Residents complained about electric buses.","Electric buses cost less than diesel buses in every respect.","Diesel buses were cheaper to maintain."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Lifetime costs were similar, with extra benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d9bb5da733","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-217","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>For years, Ines had walked past the abandoned greenhouse on her way to work, noting its broken panes and the vines pressing against the glass. One morning she stopped, pushed open the rusted door, and stepped inside. Among the weeds, a single orange tree still stood, heavy with small, bright fruit, as if it had been waiting all this time for someone to notice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ines discovers that a neglected greenhouse still holds a thriving orange tree.","The greenhouse is filled with healthy plants that someone tends daily.","Ines works in the greenhouse every morning.","Ines decides to tear down the greenhouse."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A surprising sign of life in a neglected place. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.88fcc27f3b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-218","passage":"<p>Researchers studying rats found that animals given access to running wheels learned their way through mazes faster than rats without wheels. The exercising rats also showed more new neuron growth in a brain region associated with memory. The researchers caution that rats differ from humans but suggest that exercise may support learning in other animals as well.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rats without wheels grew more neurons.","Rats that exercised learned mazes faster and grew more new neurons, suggesting exercise may aid learning.","The study proved that exercise improves human memory.","Exercise slowed rats' learning."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Faster learning and more neuron growth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d3413c0f86","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-222","passage":"<p>When an online retailer began showing delivery dates rather than shipping speeds, such as \"arrives Thursday\" instead of \"ships in three to five days,\" customers were more likely to complete purchases. Researchers suggest that a specific date is easier for shoppers to understand and plan around than a range of days.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Showing specific delivery dates instead of shipping ranges increased completed purchases, likely because dates are easier to understand.","Displaying delivery dates reduced sales.","Customers ignored delivery information.","Shoppers preferred vague shipping estimates."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Specific dates helped shoppers plan. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.27b31bb102","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-223","passage":"<p>Scientists studying a mountain lake found that its water had become clearer over several decades. At first they suspected reduced pollution. Further investigation showed that an invasive species of mussel, which filters tiny particles from the water, had spread throughout the lake. The mussels made the water clearer but also removed plankton that native fish depend on for food.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["An invasive mussel made a lake's water clearer while depleting plankton that native fish need.","Native fish benefited from the invasive mussels.","Reduced pollution was the only reason the lake became clearer.","The lake's water became cloudier over time."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Clearer water came with harm to the food web. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3b31533921","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-224","passage":"<p>Some schools have introduced \"reading buddies\" programs in which older students read with younger ones. Teachers in one school reported that younger children's reading improved, as expected. They were surprised to find that many older students who struggled with reading also showed gains, perhaps because reading aloud to younger children gave them low-pressure practice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Teachers ended the program because of poor results.","Only younger children benefited from the program.","Older students' reading declined.","A reading buddies program helped younger children and also struggling older students who read to them."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Unexpected gains for older struggling readers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.fc92c3b313","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-226","passage":"<p>Engineers testing a new type of road surface made of porous pavement found that rainwater drained through it rather than running off. During storms, streets with the new surface had less standing water, and nearby storm drains overflowed less often. In cold regions, however, water trapped in the pavement could freeze and damage it, requiring more maintenance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Porous pavement increases standing water.","The new surface works best in freezing climates.","Storm drains overflowed more with porous pavement.","Porous pavement reduced runoff and flooding but can require more maintenance in cold climates."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Drainage benefits and a cold-weather drawback. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.39f0ea5f47","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-228","passage":"<p>For many years, cities built public housing as large, isolated high-rise complexes. Critics later argued that concentrating many low-income families in one place limited access to jobs and services. More recent programs have favored mixed-income developments spread throughout neighborhoods, which some studies suggest can improve residents' access to opportunities.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Concentrated housing improved access to jobs.","Mixed-income developments have been proven harmful.","Cities continue to build only isolated high-rises.","Approaches to public housing have shifted from isolated high-rises toward mixed-income developments intended to improve residents' opportunities."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Changing approach and reasons. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.7f86677120","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-229","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The night before the move, Sara walked through the empty house one last time. Without furniture, every room sounded different, her footsteps echoing where the couch and bookshelves had once absorbed them. In her old bedroom, she pressed her hand against the wall where she had marked her height each birthday, the pencil lines still faintly visible, then turned off the light.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Sara paints over the height marks on the wall.","On her last night in her childhood home, Sara quietly revisits reminders of growing up there.","Sara is excited to leave the house immediately.","Sara's family decides not to move."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The empty rooms and height marks evoke memories. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6eb17bcbac","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data10-230","passage":"<p>Botanists studying seeds stored in a frozen seed bank found that most seeds remained capable of sprouting after decades, but the rate varied widely by species. Some seeds lost viability quickly even when frozen, suggesting that seed banks may need different storage methods, or more frequent replanting and collection, for certain plants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Every species' seeds stay viable forever when frozen.","Seed banks are unnecessary.","All seeds lose viability quickly when frozen.","Frozen seed storage works for many species but not all, suggesting some plants need different methods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Viability varied by species. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c7f08c97f0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-232","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The piano had been out of tune for years, and no one in the house played it anymore. When Aiko's grandmother moved in, she said nothing about it for a week. Then one evening, after everyone had gone to bed, the family heard music drifting up the stairs: an old, slightly sour-sounding waltz, played slowly, as if the player were remembering it one note at a time.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Aiko's grandmother quietly brings music back into the house by playing the long-neglected piano.","Aiko learns to play a waltz perfectly.","Aiko's grandmother complains that the piano is out of tune.","The family sells the old piano."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The grandmother plays the piano at night. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.48c476e94f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-234","passage":"<p>When a town replaced a busy four-way stop with a small roundabout, some residents worried that drivers would be confused. For the first few weeks, traffic moved hesitantly. Within months, however, waiting times at the intersection dropped, and the number of collisions fell, as drivers adjusted to yielding while moving slowly through the circle.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The roundabout caused more collisions over time.","A roundabout initially confused drivers but soon reduced waiting times and collisions.","Traffic moved faster in the first weeks.","Residents requested the four-way stop be restored."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Early hesitation, later improvements. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bf9a31135c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-237","passage":"<p>A study of online classes found that students who were required to post short weekly reflections about what they had learned were more likely to finish the course than students in sections without the requirement. Researchers suggest that the reflections kept students engaged and helped instructors notice early when students were struggling.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Weekly reflection posts were associated with higher course completion, possibly by increasing engagement and early support.","Reflection posts reduced course completion.","Instructors ignored the reflections.","Students disliked all online classes."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Reflections linked to finishing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.93f783853e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-239","passage":"<p>When a city began tracking which streetlights were broken using reports from residents' smartphones, repair times dropped from weeks to days. Previously, broken lights were often discovered only during periodic inspections. The reports also revealed that some neighborhoods had many more broken lights than others, prompting the city to investigate the cause.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Residents refused to report broken lights.","Periodic inspections repaired lights faster than reports.","Resident smartphone reports sped up streetlight repairs and revealed uneven problems across neighborhoods.","All neighborhoods had the same number of broken lights."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Faster repairs and new insight into patterns. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.6240d1818d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-242","passage":"<p>Some art restorers once used strong chemicals to remove old varnish from paintings, occasionally damaging the original paint beneath. Newer methods use gels that release cleaning agents slowly and can be applied to tiny areas under a microscope. These techniques allow restorers to remove grime while preserving delicate brushwork.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Strong chemicals are the safest way to restore paintings.","Gels damage paintings more than older methods.","Newer restoration methods allow grime to be removed more precisely without damaging original paint.","Restorers no longer clean paintings."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Gels offer precise, gentle cleaning. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e7cda44997","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-243","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For the whole bus ride, the new girl sat alone, staring out the window. Rosa had planned to sit with her friends, as always. But when the bus stopped at the school, Rosa noticed the girl clutching a map of the building, her hands trembling slightly. Rosa slid into the seat beside her and asked, \"Want me to show you where the gym is?\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rosa's friends ask the new girl to join them.","The new girl shows Rosa around the school.","Noticing a new student's nervousness, Rosa sets aside her usual plans to help her.","Rosa ignores the new girl for the entire day."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Rosa offers help after seeing her anxiety. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.3075dd3b42","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-246","passage":"<p>When a grocery chain began placing water bottles next to soda in refrigerated cases at checkout, sales of bottled water increased while soda sales dipped slightly. Previously, water had been stocked only in a distant aisle. The chain's analysts concluded that convenient placement influenced which drink shoppers grabbed at the last moment.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Soda sales rose sharply.","Moving water to checkout reduced water sales.","Shoppers always walk to distant aisles for water.","Placing water next to soda at checkout increased water sales, suggesting that convenient placement affects last-minute choices."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Convenience changed choices. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.25d7b679ac","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-251","passage":"<p>When a public radio station invited listeners to submit short audio stories about their neighborhoods, producers expected a few dozen entries. They received more than a thousand. Many stories came from people who had never spoken on the radio before, and several became among the station's most shared segments online.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Only professional reporters submitted stories.","Few listeners submitted stories.","The segments were rarely shared online.","A call for listener-submitted stories drew far more entries than expected, and many became popular."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Unexpected volume and popularity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d46ef44df3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data11-253","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every year on the first snowfall, Grandmother Ana made hot chocolate and told the same story about the winter she met Grandfather. The grandchildren could recite it word for word and pretended to groan. But the year Grandmother was too ill to tell it, the youngest, Pilar, stood up in front of the fire, took a deep breath, and began: \"It was the coldest winter anyone could remember...\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The grandchildren refuse to hear the story again.","Pilar forgets how the story begins.","Grandmother Ana tells a new story.","When their grandmother is too ill to tell her traditional story, the youngest grandchild continues the tradition."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Pilar takes up the story. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a7ddbe197b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-254","passage":"<p>When a city began lending air conditioners to elderly residents during heat waves, emergency room visits for heat-related illness among older adults dropped noticeably. Social workers delivering the units also checked on residents' health and connected several with other services, such as meal delivery, that they had not known about.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Heat-related emergency visits rose after the program began.","Older residents already knew about every available service.","A program lending air conditioners to older residents reduced heat-related emergency visits and connected residents with other services.","Social workers refused to deliver air conditioners."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Fewer ER visits plus additional connections. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e65720bdd1","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-256","passage":"<p>Researchers studying wild chimpanzees observed that groups in different regions use different tools and techniques, such as cracking nuts with stones in some areas but not in others where the same nuts and stones are available. Because these differences are not explained by the environment alone, some researchers describe them as cultural traditions passed down through learning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Differences in tool use among chimpanzee groups that cannot be explained by environment suggest learned cultural traditions.","Chimpanzees crack nuts only where stones are unavailable.","Tool use in chimpanzees is determined entirely by environment.","All chimpanzee groups use identical tools."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Differences persist despite similar resources. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f340b04b1c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-257","passage":"<p>When a regional airline started posting its on-time performance for each route on its website, some routes with poor records improved within months. Managers reported that public comparison motivated staff to find solutions, such as adjusting schedules that had been unrealistic and positioning spare parts at busy airports.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Spare parts were removed from busy airports.","Publicly posting on-time performance motivated airline staff to fix problems on poorly performing routes.","The airline hid its performance data.","On-time performance worsened after the data were posted."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Transparency spurred improvements. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.523620f990","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-259","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandmother spoke four languages but never learned to drive. So every Saturday, I drove her to the market across town, and every Saturday she used a different language: Portuguese with the fish seller, French with the baker, Italian with the woman who sold olives. In the car on the way home, she taught me one new word from each conversation.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The grandmother spoke only one language at the market.","The narrator refused to learn new words.","The narrator's weekly drives with her multilingual grandmother became occasions for learning words from the grandmother's many languages.","The grandmother drove the narrator to the market."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Market visits and shared language lessons. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.464f683700","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-265","passage":"<p>Scientists studying a species of songbird found that birds living on islands with fewer predators had duller feathers than birds of the same species on the mainland. Bright colors can attract mates but also make birds easier for predators to spot. The researchers suggest that the balance between attracting mates and avoiding predators differs between the two environments.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Differences in feather color between island and mainland birds may reflect different balances between attracting mates and avoiding predators.","Predators are more common on the islands.","Island birds have brighter feathers than mainland birds.","Bright colors protect birds from predators."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Color reflects trade-offs in different settings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.8cdecf61f3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-266","passage":"<p>When a public library began offering free museum passes that patrons could reserve, the passes were booked months in advance. Surveys found that many families who used them had never visited those museums because of ticket costs. Several museums reported that some pass users later returned as paying visitors or members.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Museums lost members because of the passes.","Free museum passes from a library let families who could not afford tickets visit museums, and some later became paying visitors.","Families already visited museums frequently.","The museum passes were rarely used."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Access for new visitors and later returns. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.c138500be7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-268","passage":"<p>When a regional water utility sent customers monthly reports comparing their water use with that of similar neighboring households, average water use fell by several percent. Households that had used more than their neighbors reduced their use the most. A few households using less than their neighbors, however, slightly increased their use after seeing the comparison.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Comparing households' water use with neighbors' reduced overall use, though some low users slightly increased theirs.","Water use increased for all households.","High users did not change their behavior.","The reports had no effect on water use."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Overall reduction with an unintended effect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.2801b0b1f5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data12-270","passage":"<p>A study of children's playgrounds found that equipment with some element of manageable risk, such as higher climbing structures, kept children engaged longer than very low, simple equipment. Injury rates were similar between the two types of playgrounds. Researchers suggest that appropriate challenges help children build confidence and physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Challenging equipment caused many more injuries.","Playgrounds should remove climbing structures.","Children preferred very simple equipment.","Playground equipment with manageable challenges engaged children longer without increasing injuries and may build confidence and skills."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Engagement rose; injuries did not. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.dab3985752","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-271","passage":"<p>When a small city replaced its paper parking tickets with a smartphone payment system, officials expected mainly convenience benefits. They also discovered that the data showed which blocks were almost always full and which were rarely used. The city then adjusted prices block by block, raising them where spaces were scarce and lowering them where spaces sat empty, and drivers found open spaces more easily.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The city raised prices on every block equally.","Data from a parking payment app let a city adjust prices by block, making it easier for drivers to find spaces.","Drivers stopped using the app.","The app made parking harder to find."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Usage data led to targeted pricing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.1c6622cd10","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-272","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather's toolbox had sat in the garage untouched since his funeral. When the porch step cracked, Esteban's mother reached for her phone to call a carpenter. Esteban stopped her. He opened the toolbox, found the hammer with the tape-wrapped handle, and felt his fingers settle into the worn places where his grandfather's had been.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A carpenter repairs the porch step.","Esteban uses his grandfather's tools to fix the step, feeling a physical connection to him.","Esteban's mother repairs the porch step herself.","Esteban throws away his grandfather's tools."],"answer":"B","explanation":"His hand fits the worn grip. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5ce89fcb5d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-274","passage":"<p>When a university library began staying open twenty-four hours during exam weeks, administrators expected crowded nights. Usage between midnight and six a.m. was lower than expected, but those who came reported that the quiet space helped them focus. The library kept the extended hours but reduced staffing during the least busy periods.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The library closed early during exams.","Overnight hours were extremely crowded.","A library's overnight hours during exams attracted fewer users than expected but helped those who came, so the library kept them with reduced staffing.","Students disliked the quiet space."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Lower use, but valued, so hours remained. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ad2b5db77b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-275","passage":"<p>Scientists studying the sounds of healthy forests found that dawn choruses of birds were louder and more varied in forests with greater biodiversity. In logged or degraded forests, recordings contained fewer species and longer silences. The researchers suggest that automated audio recorders could help monitor forest health across large, remote areas.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Forest soundscapes reflect biodiversity, suggesting that audio recorders could help monitor forest health.","Degraded forests produce louder bird choruses.","Logged forests contain more bird species.","Biodiversity cannot be measured with sound."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sound varies with biodiversity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ea5c937198","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-276","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I learned patience from my grandmother's bread. She would mix the dough and then do nothing at all for two hours, reading or knitting while it rose. As a child I found this unbearable and would lift the towel every ten minutes to check. \"Watching doesn't make it grow faster,\" she'd say, and pull the towel back down.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Through waiting for bread dough to rise, the narrator's grandmother taught her the value of patience.","The narrator's grandmother baked bread quickly.","The narrator never helped with baking.","Checking the dough made it rise faster."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Watching doesn't make it grow faster.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.dab449bd7e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-278","passage":"<p>Linguists studying a remote village found that elderly residents used many words for types of snow and ice that younger residents did not know. As the climate warmed and people traveled less over frozen land, the precise terms were used less often. The researchers are recording elders' speech to preserve this detailed knowledge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Younger residents know more snow words than elders.","Specialized words for snow and ice are fading in a village as conditions and activities change, prompting efforts to record them.","The climate in the village has become colder.","Researchers are teaching elders new words."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Changing environment reduces use of precise terms. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.a72c5fe3ba","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-280","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>Each autumn my mother pressed a leaf / between the pages of whatever book she read, / so that years later, opening a novel, / I'd find a maple, flat and red, / marking not a chapter / but a season of her life, / a small dry letter / from a year I barely knew.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The leaves mark chapters the mother wanted to reread.","The speaker's mother disliked autumn.","The speaker presses leaves in her own books.","The speaker finds pressed leaves in her mother's books that serve as reminders of moments in her mother's life."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Leaves mark seasons of her mother's life. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.5f0941c1a3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-281","passage":"<p>Researchers measuring air quality inside schools found that classrooms with poor ventilation had high levels of carbon dioxide by midday. Students in those rooms scored lower on tests of attention given in the afternoon. When ventilation was improved, carbon dioxide levels fell and afternoon test scores rose.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Students scored higher in poorly ventilated rooms.","Ventilation had no effect on carbon dioxide levels.","Improving classroom ventilation lowered carbon dioxide levels and was linked to better afternoon attention scores.","Carbon dioxide levels were lowest by midday."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Better air, better scores. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.26f60af369","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-283","passage":"<p>Scientists studying desert tortoises found that the animals dig burrows that can stay cool even when surface temperatures are extremely high. Other species, including snakes, lizards, and insects, often use abandoned tortoise burrows to escape the heat. The researchers describe the tortoises as important to the survival of many other desert animals.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Only tortoises use tortoise burrows.","Tortoise burrows are too hot for other animals.","Desert surface temperatures are mild.","Tortoise burrows provide cool shelter used by many other desert animals, making tortoises important to the ecosystem."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Burrows benefit many species. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.417cee47e3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-284","passage":"<p>When a hospital began allowing family members to stay overnight with patients in the intensive care unit, some staff worried that visitors would interfere with care. Instead, nurses reported that families often noticed changes in patients' conditions and alerted staff early. Patients and families also reported lower stress levels.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Nurses opposed the policy after it began.","Patients reported higher stress levels.","Families interfered with patient care.","Allowing families to stay overnight in intensive care helped staff notice changes and reduced stress, rather than interfering with care."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Families helped and stress decreased. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.bb8e357fc3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-285","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Leo had practiced his speech for the class election until he knew every word. At the podium, he looked out at his classmates, and the words vanished. After a long, awful pause, he said the only thing he could think of: \"I forgot my speech. But I remember what I wanted to say.\" Then he told them, plainly, why he wanted to help. The room was quiet when he finished, and then it applauded.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The class boos Leo's speech.","After forgetting his rehearsed speech, Leo speaks honestly and plainly, and his classmates respond with applause.","Leo delivers his memorized speech perfectly.","Leo leaves the podium without speaking."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Honest words replace the forgotten speech. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.0d143ce565","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-288","passage":"<p>Ornithologists studying crows in a city found that the birds dropped hard-shelled nuts onto crosswalks and waited for cars to crack them. The crows then retrieved the nut pieces when traffic lights stopped the cars. Researchers noted that the birds appeared to time their retrieval to the light signals.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["City crows use traffic to crack nuts and appear to time retrieving the pieces with traffic lights.","Crows crack nuts using only their beaks.","Crows avoid roads entirely.","Cars intentionally stop for crows."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Clever use of traffic and signals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.918e1153ac","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-289","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father could fix almost anything, but he rarely explained how. When I was twelve and the lawnmower broke, he handed me the manual and a screwdriver and went inside. I spent the entire afternoon covered in grease, furious. By evening the mower started. He came out, listened to it run, nodded once, and went back in. I have never been prouder of a nod.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The narrator's father taught her self-reliance by letting her fix the mower alone, and his brief approval meant a great deal to her.","The narrator failed to repair the mower.","The narrator's father fixed the mower for her.","The father praised the narrator at length."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Independence and a meaningful nod. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.e5f712f1b4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-291","passage":"<p>A study of museum visitors found that people who took photographs of artworks remembered fewer details about them later than people who simply looked. However, when visitors zoomed in on a specific part of an artwork while photographing it, their memory for that part improved. Researchers suggest that focused attention, not photography itself, affects memory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Visitors who only looked remembered nothing.","Photographing art always improves memory.","Zooming in reduced memory for details.","Taking photos generally reduced visitors' memory of artworks, but focused attention while photographing improved memory for details."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Attention, not the camera, matters. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.b2eb74ed34","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-295","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The fog rolled in every afternoon at four, as reliably as a train. Newcomers complained that it ruined their plans. The fishermen, though, set their clocks by it, pulling in their nets as the gray wall crept over the harbor. \"The fog isn't the problem,\" old Marisol liked to say. \"Expecting the sun is.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The fishermen refuse to work in fog.","While newcomers resent the daily fog, local fishermen accept it as part of life, as Marisol's saying suggests.","The fog appears at random times.","Marisol dislikes the fog more than newcomers do."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Acceptance versus frustration. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.d4c90841eb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-296","passage":"<p>When a school district replaced sugary drinks in vending machines with water and milk, researchers expected students might simply buy sodas elsewhere. Surveys found that overall soda consumption among students did decline, though some students did bring drinks from home. Consumption fell most among younger students, who had fewer opportunities to buy drinks off campus.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["All students brought soda from home.","Removing sugary drinks from school vending machines reduced soda consumption, especially among younger students.","Soda consumption increased after the change.","Older students reduced soda the most."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Overall decline, largest among younger students. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.69d843bb05","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-297","passage":"<p>When a small bookstore installed a shelf labeled \"Staff Picks\" with handwritten notes explaining why employees loved each book, sales of those titles rose sharply. Customers said the notes felt like recommendations from a friend. Several books that had been sitting unsold for months became bestsellers at the store.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Staff Picks reduced book sales.","Handwritten staff recommendations boosted sales, even for books that had not been selling.","Customers ignored the handwritten notes.","Only bestsellers were placed on the shelf."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Personal notes drove sales. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.ef09ee9f9f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-298","passage":"<p>Biologists studying salmon in a river with a newly removed dam found that fish began swimming upstream to spawning grounds they had not reached in decades. Within a few years, populations of insects, birds, and bears along the upper river also increased, as salmon carcasses and eggs provided food.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Other wildlife declined after the dam was removed.","Removing a dam allowed salmon to reach upstream spawning grounds, benefiting other wildlife along the river.","The dam removal reduced salmon populations.","Salmon had always reached the upper river."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Restored access and ecosystem benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.f073a0891a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-300","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandmother's hands / were never still: / shelling peas, / mending hems, / braiding my hair / tight enough to last a week. / Even in the hospital, / her fingers moved across the blanket, / looking for something to do.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The speaker braids the grandmother's hair.","The grandmother disliked sewing.","The grandmother rested her hands often.","The poem portrays a grandmother whose hands were always busy, even when she was ill."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Her hands \"were never still.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.2.16886f93c7","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_passages.build_CI","group":"psg-passage_data13-301","passage":"<p>When a city planted wildflower meadows along highway medians instead of mowed grass, maintenance crews mowed only once a year instead of every few weeks. The city saved money on fuel and labor, and surveys of the medians found many more bees and butterflies than before. Some drivers initially complained that the medians looked overgrown, but complaints declined as the flowers bloomed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Drivers complained more as the flowers bloomed.","Replacing mowed median grass with wildflowers cut maintenance costs and supported pollinators, and early complaints faded.","Wildflower medians required more frequent mowing.","Bees avoided the new wildflower medians."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Savings, pollinators, and declining complaints. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
